WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.125
ALEXIS MOUSSINE-POUCHKINE:
In the previous episode,

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:04.260
we realized that a Firebase
project is actually

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:06.630
a GCP project under the
hood, and that data that's

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.230
stored in Cloud Storage
can be equally accessed

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:11.700
via Firebase powered mobile
apps and web clients,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.370
as well as from GCP
hosted applications.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.190
In this episode, we'll
explore two more products--

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.608
Cloud Functions and
Cloud Firestore.

00:00:19.608 --> 00:00:20.150
Stick around.

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:23.090
[THEME MUSIC]

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.130
Cloud Functions is really
a Google Cloud product

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:31.320
which also happens to be
visible from Firebase.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.270
Cloud Functions is a
popular implementation

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:35.940
of the Functions as
a Service paradigm,

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.250
where an event in a
system, such as an HTTP

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.470
call or a file
uploaded to a bucket,

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.920
triggers the execution
of a small piece of code,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:43.682
a function.

00:00:43.682 --> 00:00:45.390
Check out the Serverless
Overview episode

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.140
for more details
on Cloud Functions.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.750
An important
architectural difference

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.810
between the Firebase and GCP
approach to Cloud Functions

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.960
is that Firebase will offer
you to wrap your functions

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.850
into callable functions.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.150
Such functions can be
invoked via the Firebase SDK

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.360
with user tokens and device
instance ideas propagated

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.519
directly to the function.

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.430
Another important difference is
the language that's supported.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.160
When using Firestore,
Node.js and TypeScript

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.090
are supported, while Go,
Python, and Java are also

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:17.850
available when using GCP
Cloud Functions directly.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.280
Before we compare web
consoles, you can also

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.360
use command line interfaces--

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.110
G Cloud or the Firebase CLI.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.570
Both come with their own
set of functionalities,

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.840
and it's recommended to decide
which one works best for you

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:31.710
and stick to it.

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.570
To deploy functions
with Firebase,

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.470
you'll need to install Firebase
command line tools using NPM,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:40.760
as deploying from the Firebase
console is not possible.

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.110
In GCP line, you
can deploy functions

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.120
straight from the
console, integrating

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.610
with GCP's private
source repositories,

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.340
or even typing the function
source code in line.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.430
Firebase command line tools
do, however, come with an API

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.530
for strongly typed handling
of some trigger events,

00:01:56.530 --> 00:01:59.070
and with the ability to deploy
multiple functions at once.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.290
Just as you would
expect, a Cloud Function

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.750
created with a Firebase
project can also

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:05.520
be managed using Cloud Console.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.570
In fact, clicking on Detailed
Usage Stats in Firebase

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:10.500
will take you to
that same function,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.360
only in Cloud
Console, where you'll

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.510
get additional features, such
as monitoring graphs, a tab

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.490
to test the function,
and the ability

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:20.040
to set features such
as retry on failure,

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.440
memory allocation, or timeouts.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.110
Whichever way you use Cloud
Functions in your Firebase

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:27.330
or GCP architecture,
they're likely to be

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.490
very effective at
what they do best--

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:32.220
small tasks in reaction
to all sorts of events.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.880
Cloud Firestore is Google's
state of the art NoSQL document

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:37.160
database.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.350
It is schema-less and offers
to store documents containing

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:43.050
attributes in a
hierarchy of collections.

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:43.830
It is fast.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.480
It supports transactions.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:47.870
It's highly available,
strongly consistent,

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:50.520
and it does not require managing
any kind of infrastructure.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.880
Some will say it's a
serverless database.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:55.530
Cloud Firestore comes
in two flavors--

00:02:55.530 --> 00:02:59.100
datastore mode, which is mostly
suitable for server projects,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:01.530
and native mode for
mobile and web apps.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.260
Let me focus here on
the native mode, which

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:06.810
is the one that is common
to Firebase and GCP users.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.790
This mode can
automatically scale

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:10.960
to millions of
concurrent clients,

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.630
but what makes it
even more special

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:16.240
is the fact that it offers
near real time notifications.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.940
This is what enables
synchronization

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.860
of data across devices.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.260
With its built-in
offline support,

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.870
you can access and make
changes to your data,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.300
and those changes will
be sent to the cloud when

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:29.010
the client comes back online.

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:31.020
Data stored in
Cloud Firestore can

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.840
be accessed using Firebase
SDKs, and of course, you

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.470
can use either console to view
and edit data and indices,

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.600
and to monitor
database access usage.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.930
With Cloud Firestore, you can
query the database directly

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.570
from your mobile or web
clients without the need

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.510
to set up an intermediary server
to manage access to your data,

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.110
and this is why the Firebase
console has an additional tab

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.480
for security access roles.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.490
If you are a Firebase
user shipping

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.010
an app that accesses
Firestore, then you really

00:03:59.010 --> 00:04:01.020
should use Firebase
Authentication

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.810
and think carefully about
your security access rules.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.780
On the GCP side, there are
no equivalent security rules,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:09.510
as you typically access
Firestore using a service

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:10.500
account.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.240
Generally speaking,
server side code

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.640
is considered trusted,
as opposed to client code

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:15.900
from mobile apps.

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.819
Finally, supported Firestore
SDK languages include Python,

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:24.960
Node.js, Java, C#,
.NET, Go, PHP, and Ruby,

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.370
with Firebase mobile SDKs adding
web, Android, and iOS support.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:32.340
These mobile Firebase
SDKs include local caching

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:34.020
as a unique feature
to help implement

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.630
the offline capability
that I mentioned earlier--

00:04:36.630 --> 00:04:38.550
same project and
billing infrastructure,

00:04:38.550 --> 00:04:40.980
common Cloud Storage,
Cloud Functions,

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:43.050
and Cloud Firestore services.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.420
The two platforms
have a lot in common.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.640
So whether you're
a GCP developer

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.520
interested in building mobile
and web apps with Firebase,

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.220
or a Firebase user trying
to grow your usage of GCP

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:56.580
services, you should now have a
good sense of what's possible.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.210
It's time to get going with
Firebase or GCP or both.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:01.660
Thanks for watching this video.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.410
Please like, subscribe,
comment, share, and look forward

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.820
to more GCP Essentials videos.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.870
[THEME MUSIC]

